## Upstream weather models

Open-Meteo's licence page states it "utilises open-data from various national weather services including" DWD, ECMWF, NOAA NCEP, Meteo-France, JMA, the Norwegian Meteorological Institute and CMC, each with its own linked licence. SkyWire credits Open-Meteo, which is the party that licenses the API output to us. We read that listing and did not open each national licence, so nothing is claimed here about their individual terms.

## Tier and limits

SkyWire runs the free keyless Open-Meteo tier for both the forecast API and the geocoding API. That tier is non-commercial only. The terms page states "You may only use the free API services for non-commercial purposes". The pricing table marks commercial use unavailable on Free / Open-Access and available on API Standard, API Professional and API Enterprise. A subscription grants "a commercial use licence and an API key for the dedicated customer endpoint" at customer-api.open-meteo.com.

Free tier call limits, quoted from the terms: "Less than 10'000 API calls per day, 5'000 per hour and 600 per minute." The pricing page adds a cap of 300,000 per month. Requests covering more than 10 weather variables or more than 2 weeks count as more than one call. The page says monthly limits are not enforced yet. Open-Meteo reserves "the right to block applications and IP addresses that misuse our service without prior notice".

GeoNames free web services are capped at "10'000 credits daily limit per application (identified by the parameter 'username'), the hourly limit is 1000 credits". SkyWire does not use those services.

The three SkyWire descriptors declare `rate_limit_per_sec: 20`. Each miner request makes two upstream calls, three when a wind question or a morning window also pulls the hourly series. Sustained traffic at the declared rate would exceed the 600 per minute free ceiling.
